# Secret-adjacent files, and the BACKUP names tooling tends to write beside them.
#
# From the hook session's rotation incident (2026-08-30): a script wrote `.env`
# backups as `.env.bak-<stamp>` beside the source, and that repo's `.gitignore`
# listed `.env`, `.env.local` and `.env.*.local` -- none of which match. Three
# untracked files holding the CURRENT LIVE key sat in a public working tree; one
# `git add -A` would have published a working credential. Nothing reached GitHub.
#
# `.env.*` already covers that case here, verified with `git check-ignore`. These
# add the shapes it does NOT cover: generic editor/tool backups, and key material
# by extension. Latent rather than active -- nothing here generates them today --
# which is the point of adding them before something does.
#
# Rule worth keeping: check `git check-ignore` on the BACKUP name, not the source.
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.orig
*.rej
*~
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ed25519
*.keystore
secrets.json
credentials.json

# Node dependencies, at the ROOT so it covers EVERY subdirectory.
# 2026-09-15: 783 files / 34.9 MB of `integrations/lucid/node_modules` were
# committed -- a third of the repo -- because only two integrations carried
# their own rule and a `git add -A` inside a rebase-resolution loop staged the
# present working tree against a commit whose .gitignore predated the
# directory. Stripped with filter-branch; this rule is why the next TS
# integration cannot repeat it.
node_modules/
.vite/
